#: g101/3d/101_3d_06.md
msgid ""
"For the ramp, we'll use a `CSGPolygon`. This CSG shape lets you extrude a "
"given\n"
"polygon to a desired depth. The default shape is a square, but you can add "
"or\n"
"remove points. For a ramp, we want 3 points."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_06.md
msgid ""
"* **Union** - The shapes will be merged, removing any \"inside\" geometry.\n"
"* **Intersection** - Only the intersecting (overlapping) geometry will be "
"kept.\n"
"* **Subtraction** - The second shape is \"cut out\" of the first."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_06.md
msgid ""
"CSG is a powerful tool for building objects directly in Godot without\n"
"having to move to another modeling application such as Blender. It can be "
"helpful\n"
"if you need to mock up a test level for your game, or for the final\n"
"environment. Keep in mind that as the CSG tree becomes more complex, it can\n"
"incur a performance penalty. Try to use CSGCombiner to separate your scene\n"
"into separate CSG trees to minimize this."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_06.md
msgid ""
"Hopefully you're already seeing the possibilities of creating complex "
"shapes\n"
"through these 3 operations."
msgstr ""

