#: physics/Godot3_KYN_rigidbody1.md
msgid ""
"<a href=\"http://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/classes/"
"class_rigidbody2d.html\" target=\"_blank\"><svg width=\"18\" height=\"18\" "
"class=\"icon-icon_rigid_body_2d\">\n"
"<use xlink:href=\"/blog/img/symbol-defs.svg#icon-icon_rigid_body_2d\"></svg> "
"`RigidBody2D`</a>\n"
"is the physics body in Godot that provides simulated physics. This means "
"that you\n"
"don't control a RigidBody2D directly. Instead you apply forces to it "
"(gravity, impulses,\n"
"etc.) and Godot's built-in physics engine calculates the resulting movement, "
"including\n"
"collisions, bouncing, rotating, etc."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_KYN_rigidbody1.md
msgid ""
"By default, the physics settings provide some _damping_, which reduces a "
"body's\n"
"velocity and spin. In space, there's no friction, so there shouldn't be any\n"
"damping at all. However, for the \"Asteroids\" feel, we want the ship to "
"stop rotating\n"
"when we let go of the keys, so set the ship's `Angular -> Damp` to `5`."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_KYN_rigidbody1.md
msgid ""
"To quote the [RigidBody2D docs](http://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/"
"classes/class_rigidbody2d.html#description):"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_KYN_rigidbody1.md
msgid ""
"{{< highlight swift >}}\n"
"func _integrate_forces(state):\n"
"    set_applied_force(thrust.rotated(rotation))\n"
"    set_applied_torque(rotation_dir * spin_thrust)\n"
"    var xform = state.get_transform()\n"
"    if xform.origin.x > screensize.x:\n"
"        xform.origin.x = 0\n"
"    if xform.origin.x < 0:\n"
"        xform.origin.x = screensize.x\n"
"    if xform.origin.y > screensize.y:\n"
"        xform.origin.y = 0\n"
"    if xform.origin.y < 0:\n"
"        xform.origin.y = screensize.y\n"
"    state.set_transform(xform)\n"
"{{< /highlight >}}"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_KYN_rigidbody1.md
msgid ""
"In this tutorial, I'll explain when (and when not) to use rigid bodies, how "
"they\n"
"work, and demonstrate a few handy tricks to bend them to your will. The\n"
"examples will use RigidBody2D, but the lessons apply equally to 3D."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_KYN_rigidbody1.md
msgid ""
"To cause a rigid body to move, it must have some velocity. You can give the "
"body\n"
"an initial velocity using the `Linear -> Velocity` property. Try setting "
"this\n"
"to `(500, 0)`."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_KYN_rigidbody1.md
msgid ""
"Let's walk through what this script is doing. The two variables, "
"`engine_thrust`\n"
"and `spin_thrust` control how fast the ship can accelerate and turn. In the\n"
"Inspector, set them to `500` and `25000` respectively (the units of torque "
"make\n"
"for large numbers). `thrust` will represent the ship's engine state: `(0, "
"0)`\n"
"when coasting, or a vector with the length of `engine_thrust` when powered "
"on.\n"
"`rotation_dir` will represent what direction the ship is turning. The "
"`screensize`\n"
"variable will capture the size of the screen, which we'll be using later."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_KYN_rigidbody1.md
msgid ""
"> You should not change a RigidBody2D’s position or linear_velocity every "
"frame or even very often. If you need to directly affect the body’s state, "
"use `_integrate_forces`, which allows you to directly access the physics "
"state."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_KYN_rigidbody1.md
msgid ""
"Now create a Main scene (I usually use a <a href=\"http://"
"docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/classes/class_node.html\" "
"target=\"_blank\"><svg width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"icon-"
"icon_node\"><use xlink:href=\"/blog/img/symbol-defs.svg#icon-icon_node\"></"
"svg>Node</a>) for the root).\n"
"Add a few <a href=\"http://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/classes/"
"class_staticbody2d.html\" target=\"_blank\"><svg width=\"18\" height=\"18\" "
"class=\"icon-icon_staticbody2d\"><use xlink:href=\"/blog/img/symbol-"
"defs.svg#icon-icon_static_body_2d\"></svg>StaticBody2D</a>\n"
"nodes with rectangular collision shapes to serve as your \"ground\" and "
"walls."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_KYN_rigidbody1.md
msgid ""
"So there's our answer. Instead of using `_physics_process()` we need to use "
"`_integrate_forces()`,\n"
"which gives us access to the <a href=\"http://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/"
"classes/class_physics2ddirectbodystate.html\" "
"target=\"_blank\">Physics2DDirectBodyState</a>. I highly recommend you take "
"a look at the\n"
"linked document, there is a lot of really useful data provided in the "
"physics state object.\n"
"For our purposes, the key piece of information is the body's <a "
"href=\"http://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/classes/"
"class_transform2d.html\" target=\"_blank\">Transform2D</a>.\n"
"(Explaining transforms is beyond the scope of this document - see [Matrices "
"and transforms](http://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/learning/features/math/"
"matrices_and_transforms.html)\n"
"for more information.)"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_KYN_rigidbody1.md
msgid ""
"Next, the `input()` function captures the keystates and sets the ship's "
"`thrust`\n"
"on or off, and the rotation direction (`rotation_dir`) positive or negative. "
"This\n"
"function is called every frame in `_process()`."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_KYN_rigidbody1.md
msgid ""
"{{< highlight swift >}}\n"
"extends RigidBody2D"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_KYN_rigidbody1.md
