#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"This is the node that implements simulated 2D physics. You do not control a "
"rigid body directly, but instead you apply forces to it (gravity, impulses, "
"etc.) and the physics engine calculates the resulting movement. See [Godot "
"3.0: Rigid Bodies](/blog/2017/12/godot3_kyn_rigidbody1/) for more "
"information."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/3.x/img/k2d_platf_sample.gif?width=300)"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid "Using KinematicBody2D"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"This happens because there is no _collision response_. `move_and_collide()` "
"just stops\n"
"the body's movement when a collision occurs. We need to code whatever "
"response we\n"
"want from the collision."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"In game development you often need to know when two objects in the game "
"space intersect or come into contact. This is known as _collision "
"detection_. When a collision is detected, you typically want something to "
"happen. This is known as _collision response_."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"|           |Layers|Mask|\n"
"|-----------|------|----|\n"
"|**Player** |`1`|`2, 3` |\n"
"|**Enemy**  |`2`|`1`    |\n"
"|**Coin**   |`3`|`1`    |"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"func get_input():\n"
"\t# add these actions in Project Settings -> Input Map\n"
"\tvelocity = Vector2()\n"
"\tif Input.is_action_pressed('backward'):\n"
"\t\tvelocity = Vector2(-speed/3, 0).rotated(rotation)\n"
"\tif Input.is_action_pressed('forward'):\n"
"\t\tvelocity = Vector2(speed, 0).rotated(rotation)\n"
"\tif Input.is_action_just_pressed('mouse_click'):\n"
"\t\tshoot()"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"{{< highlight python>}}\n"
"var collision = move_and_collide(velocity * delta)\n"
"if collision:\n"
"\tvelocity = velocity.slide(collision.normal)\n"
"{{< /highlight >}}\n"
"{{< highlight python>}}\n"
"velocity = move_and_slide(velocity)\n"
"{{< /highlight >}}"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"func change_state(new_state):\n"
"    state = new_state\n"
"    match state:\n"
"        IDLE:\n"
"            new_anim = 'idle'\n"
"        RUN:\n"
"            new_anim = 'run'\n"
"        JUMP:\n"
"            new_anim = 'jump_up'"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"When using `move_and_slide()` the function returns a vector representing "
"the\n"
"movement that remained after the slide collision occurred. Setting that "
"value back\n"
"to the character's `velocity` allows us to smoothly move up and down slopes. "
"Try\n"
"removing `velocity =` and see what happens if you don't do this."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"A common question from new Godot users is: \"How do you decide which "
"movement function to use?\"\n"
"Often the response is to use `move_and_slide()` because it's \"simpler\", "
"but this is not\n"
"necessarily the case. One way to think of it is that `move_and_slide()` is a "
"special case,\n"
"and `move_and_collide()` is more general. For example, the following two "
"code snippets result in the same collision response:"
