#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid "#### move_and_slide"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"func get_input():\n"
"    velocity.x = 0\n"
"    var right = Input.is_action_pressed('ui_right')\n"
"    var left = Input.is_action_pressed('ui_left')\n"
"    var jump = Input.is_action_just_pressed('ui_select')"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid "You have three nodes with the following configuration:"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"{{< gd-icon KinematicBody2D >}}`KinematicBody2D` is for implementing bodies "
"that are to be controlled via code. They detect collisions with other bodies "
"when moving, but are not affected by engine physics properties like gravity "
"or friction. While this means that you have to write some code to create "
"their behavior, it also means you have more precise control over how they "
"move and react."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"We're using a very rudimentary state machine to handle the transitions "
"between the character's idle, running, and jumping states."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"export (PackedScene) var Bullet\n"
"export (int) var speed"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"func _physics_process(delta):\n"
"\tvar collision = move_and_collide(velocity * delta)\n"
"\tif collision:\n"
"\t\tvelocity = velocity.bounce(collision.normal)\n"
"\t\tif collision.collider.has_method(\"hit\"):\n"
"\t\t\tcollision.collider.hit()"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"func _physics_process(delta):\n"
"    velocity.y += gravity * delta\n"
"    if state == JUMP:\n"
"        if is_on_floor():\n"
"            change_state(IDLE)\n"
"    velocity = move_and_slide(velocity, Vector2(0, -1))"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"Run the scene again and try moving into the obstacle. You'll see that the "
"{{< gd-icon KinematicBody2D >}}`KinematicBody2D`\n"
"can't penetrate the obstacle. However, try moving into the obstacle at an "
"angle and\n"
"you'll find that the obstacle acts like glue - it feels like the body gets "
"stuck."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/3.x/img/k2d_layer_example.png?width=300)"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"Also note that we've added `Vector2(0, -1)` as the floor normal. This is a "
"vector\n"
"pointing straight upward. This means that if the character collides with an "
"object\n"
"that has this normal, it will be considered a floor."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid "Let's look at each of the properties in turn:"
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"Run this scene and you'll see that `move_and_collide()` works as expected, "
"moving\n"
"the body along the velocity vector. Now let's see what happens when you add\n"
"some obstacles. Add a {{< gd-icon StaticBody2D >}}`StaticBody2D` with a "
"rectangular collision shape. For visibility,\n"
"you can use a sprite, a {{< gd-icon Polygon2D >}}`Polygon2D`, or just turn "
"on \"Visible Collision Shapes\" from\n"
"the \"Debug\" menu."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"The `move_and_slide()` method is intended to simplify the collision response "
"in the common case where you want one body to slide along the other. This is "
"especially useful in platformers or top-down games, for example."
msgstr ""

#: physics/Godot3_kinematic2d.md
msgid ""
"if jump and is_on_floor():\n"
"        change_state(JUMP)\n"
"        velocity.y = jump_speed\n"
"    if right:\n"
"        change_state(RUN)\n"
"        velocity.x += run_speed\n"
"    if left:\n"
"        change_state(RUN)\n"
"        velocity.x -= run_speed\n"
"    $Sprite.flip_h = velocity.x < 0\n"
"    if !right and !left and state == RUN:\n"
"        change_state(IDLE)"
